      "problem": "John has $3$ pants, $5$ shirts, and $6$ pairs of socks.\nIf John wears one pair of pants, one shirt, and one matching pair of socks, how many different combinations of outfits can he wear?",
      "worked_solution": "Using the **rule of product**, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Number of outfits} & =\\text{Number of pants} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of shirts} \\\\  & \\times \\text{Number of pairs of socks} \\\\  & =3\\times 5\\times 6 \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, John can dress in $90$ different ways.",
